WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.400
It's crazy to say out loud, but we've been with&nbsp;
each other for 17 years now. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.640
we've had plenty of discussions on the topic&nbsp;
of children. Do we want to be parents? Is life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.480
better without kids? Would there be any regret&nbsp;
if we didn't have children? Looking at our life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.760
through social media, it might be easy to&nbsp;
assume we are DINKs (Dual income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.040
no kids). We have even been asked to do&nbsp;
interviews about being DINKs. But to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.120
many family planning questions we have gotten over&nbsp;
the years, we have always said maybe someday we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.680
will have kids. Then life happened. Before we knew&nbsp;
it, we have already crossed into what's medically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.000
considered advanced maternal age. Suddenly,&nbsp;
looking at the trajectory of our lives, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.320
like we will remain as DINKs after all, and we&nbsp;
were genuinely fine with that. To be child-free or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.720
have children, both seemed like perfectly valid&nbsp;
choices, each with their own advantages. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.960
as usual on the internet, the discourse&nbsp;
between the child-free and pro-parenthood camps is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:16.320
much more polarized. Agree or disagree? Choosing&nbsp;
not to have children is fundamentally selfish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.880
First of all, I think people tend to conflate&nbsp;
selfishness and self-interest. So in that sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:32.480
if we use the terms loosely, then I would actually&nbsp;
agree because I believe outside of exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.600
cases when normal people like you and I when&nbsp;
we decide whether or not we want to have kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.000
is almost always from a self-interested point&nbsp;
of view. Our decision can be ultimately summed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.960
up with one question. Do we want a life with&nbsp;
children or do we want a life without children?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.600
And we acknowledge that either of those options&nbsp;
would be out of self-interest. It's based on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.560
we want. And we won't pretend we are doing it for&nbsp;
some selfless reason. So if somebody says, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.800
I actually really want kids. I just choose not&nbsp;
to to save the environment." Or "Oh, I don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.400
want kids. I'm just doing it for the economy."&nbsp;
Personally, I'll be very skeptical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:26.480
Maybe altruistic reasons can play a minor role in&nbsp;
influencing a person's decisions to have kids or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:34.960
not, but to me those have always sounded more like&nbsp;
retrospective rationalizations or justifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:42.240
for a personal decision that was much more&nbsp;
influenced by selfish decisions. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:49.680
because the choice of being child-free or to have&nbsp;
children is such a personal topic, accusations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.040
start getting thrown around. You're selfish for&nbsp;
not having children. No, you are the one who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:02.160
selfish for having children. Guys, it's simple.&nbsp;
You're all selfish. We are all selfish, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:09.760
okay. It's unethical to bring children&nbsp;
into the world given the current state it's in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.720
That's just kind of a pessimistic way to look&nbsp;
at it, right? I mean, our grandparents went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:25.200
through world wars, so surely the world wasn't&nbsp;
in a better state back then, but they had kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.320
and that's why we're here today.&nbsp;
And I wouldn't say that they were being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:37.120
unethical or irresponsible. Of course, there's&nbsp;
a lot of s*** going on in the world all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:44.560
but I think most of us would still agree that&nbsp;
life is overall still worth living. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:52.080
also feel like whatever's going on out there in&nbsp;
the world, which isn't always in our control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.160
is going to have less of an impact compared&nbsp;
to the immediate environment a child is in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.480
which we have much more control over. And that's&nbsp;
what would make a bigger difference in whether a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:11.680
child grows up happy and resilient.&nbsp;
But if someone has thought about it and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.920
think it's an uphill battle and they're&nbsp;
not confident that they can provide the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.920
environment that would overcome all the s*** that's&nbsp;
going on out there, that's why they've decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:30.480
the most ethical thing for them to do is&nbsp;
to not have children, then fair enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:37.840
They've thought it through and I think they made&nbsp;
a perfectly valid decision for themselves. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.920
still wouldn't agree that bringing children into&nbsp;
this world in its current state is inherently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.960
unethical or irresponsible. People who don't&nbsp;
experience parenthood are missing out on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:05:01.440
fundamental human experience. I would actually&nbsp;
agree. Although it depends on what it means by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:09.200
fundamental human experience. But then again,&nbsp;
I feel like under most reasonable definitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:17.120
raising children would be significant &nbsp;
and defining enough to qualify as a central human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:25.600
experience. However, I'm not suggesting a person's&nbsp;
life will be incomplete or less fulfilling if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.800
don't have children. I don't think those are&nbsp;
the same things. You can miss out on a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:37.760
human experience and still find fulfillment, maybe&nbsp;
even more fulfillment somewhere else. Another way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:45.280
I would think about it is, I believe most people&nbsp;
would say love is central to the human experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.920
There are different forms of love, of course,&nbsp;
but the ones that immediately come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:56.640
mind are romantic love or family love and&nbsp;
of course the love that's unique to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:04.720
a parent child relationship. But for example,&nbsp;
someone could have never fallen in love and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:10.720
might say they are missing out on that particular&nbsp;
aspect of the human experience. And even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:17.760
then, they can definitely still live a complete and&nbsp;
 fulfilling life. So at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:24.720
if we didn't have children and someone said to me,&nbsp;
 you're missing out on a fundamental human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:31.280
experience, I wouldn't disagree. But that&nbsp;
wouldn't take anything away from the life that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:38.880
have lived. No one can experience everything life&nbsp;
has to offer. Some people want certain experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:47.600
and some people don't. And that's it. Do I 
want that experience? Actually, I do. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.440
there's the question of whether I'm ready to be&nbsp;
a mother. I can be a pretty anxious person. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.880
I like certainty. I would want everything&nbsp;
planned out and in perfect order before I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.880
feel ready to commit. But our lives are not&nbsp;
exactly conventional and parenthood is wildly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.320
unpredictable. I realized the certainty I was&nbsp;
hoping for doesn't really exist. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:16.240
more than anything, I just really look forward to&nbsp;
experiencing parenthood together with my partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:22.400
who I've always thought would be a fantastic dad.&nbsp;
Now age is not on our side. The best time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:28.280
to have kids has passed. The second best time&nbsp;
would be now. So earlier this year, we &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:34.880
thought, why not start trying? After all, things&nbsp;
might take a while or it might not happen at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:41.440
But to our surprise, we didn't have to wait long.&nbsp;
And I'm extremely grateful and excited for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:47.440
But I'll be lying if I said I didn't feel a sense&nbsp;
of anxiety kick in because of how quickly it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:53.200
happened. I'm just thankful that my child will&nbsp;
be in calm and safe hands even if I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:08:02.240
flustered along the way. Agree or disagree? I'm&nbsp;
ready to be a parent. I don't know if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:10.640
I'll ever feel 100% ready to be a parent,&nbsp;
but I'm ready to adapt. This is going to be one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:16.280
heck of an experience for sure. I'm going&nbsp;
to have to adapt and I can't wait.

